; ======================================================================
;  pwm.pi4 - the BCM2711 pulse width modulators, and a coarse software
;  PWM for a pin that is not one of theirs.
;
;  WHAT THIS FILE OWNS
;
;    * PWM0 at $FE20C000 and PWM1 at $FE20C800, two channels each
;      (BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 8.6: "The PWM0 register base
;      address is 0x7e20c000 and the PWM1 register base address is
;      0x7e20c800", translated by the device tree's own soc ranges,
;      Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:40 - <0x7e000000 0x0 0xfe000000>).
;    * clk_pwm, which is NOT in the PWM block. Section 8.5's last line
;      says so in four words: "PWM clock source and frequency is
;      controlled in CPRMAN." So this file also programs CM_PWMCTL and
;      CM_PWMDIV in the clock manager at $FE101000.
;    * one SOFTWARE channel, a level toggled from a periodic call, for a
;      transistor on any GPIO. It is coarse and it says so everywhere.
;
;  WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN. The fan curve (Anvil/Core/fan.pbi), the pin the
;  operator chose and the words printed about any of it (the `fan`
;  command). This file takes a pin number and a frequency and produces
;  a waveform, or a named refusal.
;
;  REQUIRES, and the main file lists both in this order (house rule 10 -
;  a library never includes a library):
;
;      XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"     ; PinAlt, PinHigh...
;      XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"    ; Micros
;      XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pwm.pi4"
;
;  THIS FILE HAS DRIVEN A PIN ON SILICON (2026-09-06, pi4-fan-silicon,
;  a four-wire fan on GPIO 18, PWM0 channel 1 through ALT5, 25 kHz).
;  `gpio status 18` reads level 0 at a duty of 0, level 1 at 1000, and
;  either value on successive reads at 500. The line this replaces said
;  NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS DRIVEN A PIN ON SILICON, and it was right:
;  the first version of this file reported every number correctly and
;  left the pad low. What it was missing is written out under THE
;  SILICON FACT below - read that before changing anything about the
;  clock or the control byte.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE TWO MODES, AND WHY THIS FILE USES M/S
; ======================================================================
;  Section 8.4 gives PWM mode two sub-modes. With MSEN=0 the channel
;  runs the "evenly distributed pulses" algorithm of section 8.3 - it
;  spreads N ones through M cycles, so the output flips at close to
;  clk_pwm and the ratio is right only when averaged. With MSEN=1 it
;  sends M cycles high then S-M low: one edge pair per period, at
;  exactly clk_pwm/RNG.
;
;  A FOUR-PIN FAN NEEDS THE SECOND. Its control input is specified as a
;  25 kHz square wave whose DUTY it measures; a 54 MHz stream of evenly
;  distributed pulses with the same average is not that signal, and the
;  fan's own tachometer and control loop are entitled to do anything at
;  all with it. So every channel this file starts sets MSENi, which is
;  also what the downstream kernel driver does unconditionally
;  (Datasheets/pi4/pwm/rpi-6.12.y_pwm-bcm2835.c:19, `#define PWM_MODE
;  0x80  /* set timer in PWM mode */`, ORed into every channel's control
;  byte in bcm2835_pwm_apply) - 0x80 is bit 7 of the byte, which is
;  MSEN1 in Table 153, so that comment names the sub-mode rather than
;  the mode.
;
;  The FIFO is not used (USEFi stays 0) and neither is serialiser mode
;  (MODEi stays 0). A fan wants one number until told otherwise, which
;  is exactly what DATi is for.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PWM BLOCKS.
;
;  Two instances, "named PWM0 and PWM1 (each with two output channels)"
;  - section 8.2. The device tree names both: bcm283x.dtsi:413
;  pwm@7e20c000 and bcm2711.dtsi:275 pwm1@7e20c800, each with a reg size
;  of 0x28, which is the register map below plus its last word.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM0_BASE = $FE20C000
#PWM1_BASE = $FE20C800

; Table 152, the PWM register map. Offsets are from the block's base and
; are the same for PWM0 and PWM1.
#PWM_CTL_OFF  = $00           ; PWM Control
#PWM_STA_OFF  = $04           ; PWM Status
#PWM_DMAC_OFF = $08           ; PWM DMA Configuration - untouched here
#PWM_RNG1_OFF = $10           ; Channel 1 Range
#PWM_DAT1_OFF = $14           ; Channel 1 Data
#PWM_FIF1_OFF = $18           ; FIFO input - untouched here
#PWM_RNG2_OFF = $20           ; Channel 2 Range
#PWM_DAT2_OFF = $24           ; Channel 2 Data

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CTL, Table 153. The control register holds one BYTE per channel:
;  channel 1 in bits 7:0 and channel 2 in bits 15:8, the same field in
;  the same position eight bits up. That is why the kernel driver writes
;  it as `PWM_CONTROL_SHIFT(x) ((x) * 8)` with a 0xff mask
;  (pwm-bcm2835.c:16-18) and why the constants below are the CHANNEL 1
;  positions, shifted by 8 for channel 2.
;
;  ONE BIT BREAKS THAT SYMMETRY AND IT IS THE TRAP: bit 6 is CLRF, "Clear
;  FIFO", and it is NOT per-channel - it is one shared one-shot for the
;  single FIFO. Bit 14, where a per-channel reading would put channel
;  2's copy, is Reserved. So CLRF has its own absolute constant below
;  and is never shifted. Writing it would clear a FIFO this file does
;  not use, which is harmless and still wrong, so it is never written.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM_CTL_PWEN = $01           ; bit 0  PWEN1 - channel enable
#PWM_CTL_MODE = $02           ; bit 1  MODE1 - 0 PWM, 1 serialiser
#PWM_CTL_RPTL = $04           ; bit 2  RPTL1 - repeat last FIFO word
#PWM_CTL_SBIT = $08           ; bit 3  SBIT1 - the silence level
#PWM_CTL_POLA = $10           ; bit 4  POLA1 - invert the output
#PWM_CTL_USEF = $20           ; bit 5  USEF1 - transmit from the FIFO
#PWM_CTL_MSEN = $80           ; bit 7  MSEN1 - M/S instead of the
                              ;        distributed-pulse algorithm
#PWM_CTL_CLRF = $40           ; bit 6  CLRF, SHARED, never shifted
#PWM_CTL_CHAN_SHIFT = 8       ; channel 2's byte sits this far up
#PWM_CTL_CHAN_MASK  = $FF     ; and is this wide

; STA, Table 154. Only the two error flags this file can actually cause
; are named; the FIFO flags belong to a FIFO nobody here fills.
#PWM_STA_BERR  = $100         ; bit 8  bus error - a write went too fast
#PWM_STA_STA1  = $200         ; bit 9  channel 1 is transmitting
#PWM_STA_STA2  = $400         ; bit 10 channel 2 is transmitting

; RNG reset value, Table 156: 0x00000020. Worth naming because a channel
; enabled without setting RNG runs at clk_pwm/32, which at the 54 MHz
; oscillator is 1.6875 MHz - a real waveform, on the wrong pin's fan, at
; a frequency nothing asked for.
#PWM_RNG_RESET = $20

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CLOCK MANAGER.
;
;  Base $FE101000. Section 5.4.2 states the bus address for the general
;  purpose clocks - "The General Purpose clocks register base address is
;  0x7e101000" - and the device tree gives the whole block: bcm283x.dtsi
;  :86 `cprman@7e101000` with reg size 0x2000.
;
;  THE PWM CLOCK'S OWN TWO REGISTERS ARE NOT IN THE DATASHEET. Section
;  5.4 documents CM_GP0CTL/DIV through CM_GP2CTL/DIV at 0x70..0x84 and
;  stops; the per-peripheral clocks are undocumented there. Their
;  offsets come from the downstream kernel's clock driver, which is what
;  actually programs this block on this part:
;  Datasheets/pi4/pwm/rpi-6.12.y_clk-bcm2835.c:86-87
;      #define CM_PWMCTL   0x0a0
;      #define CM_PWMDIV   0x0a4
;  and its clock table confirms the pairing at line 2212:
;      [BCM2835_CLOCK_PWM] = REGISTER_PER_CLK(.name = "pwm",
;          .ctl_reg = CM_PWMCTL, .div_reg = CM_PWMDIV,
;          .int_bits = 12, .frac_bits = 12, .is_mash_clock = true)
;
;  THE BIT LAYOUT IS THE DATASHEET'S. Tables 99 and 100 define
;  CM_GP*CTL and CM_GP*DIV, and the per-peripheral pair uses the same
;  layout - the kernel's general CM_* bit constants (clk-bcm2835.c:124-
;  136) are applied to every ctl_reg in that table, including this one.
;  So both sources agree field for field, and the two are cited
;  separately below wherever they say the same thing twice.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CM_BASE       = $FE101000
#CM_PWMCTL_OFF = $A0
#CM_PWMDIV_OFF = $A4

; Bits 31:24 of BOTH registers are PASSWD, "Clock Manager password 5a"
; (Tables 99 and 100), and a write without it is discarded. The kernel
; spells the same thing as a whole word: clk-bcm2835.c:41
; `#define CM_PASSWORD 0x5a000000`.
#CM_PASSWD = $5A000000

; CM_*CTL fields. Table 99 for SRC/ENAB/KILL/BUSY/MASH, and
; clk-bcm2835.c:124-131 for the same positions plus GATE and BUSYD,
; which Table 99 leaves Reserved and blank respectively.
#CM_CTL_SRC_MASK = $0F        ; bits 3:0
#CM_CTL_ENAB     = $10        ; bit 4  request start/stop, glitch-free
#CM_CTL_KILL     = $20        ; bit 5  stop and reset - test/debug only
#CM_CTL_GATE     = $40        ; bit 6  Reserved in Table 99; BIT(6) in
                              ;        the kernel, which sets it with
                              ;        ENABLE in bcm2835_clock_on
                              ;        (clk-bcm2835.c:1111-1114)
#CM_CTL_BUSY     = $80        ; bit 7  the generator is running
#CM_CTL_BUSYD    = $100       ; bit 8
#CM_CTL_MASH_SHIFT = 9        ; bits 10:9, Table 99: 0 = integer division

; SRC, Table 99: 0 GND, 1 oscillator, 2/3 test-debug, 4 PLLA per,
; 5 PLLC per, 6 PLLD per, 7 HDMI auxiliary, 8-15 GND. Same numbering in
; clk-bcm2835.c:134-145.
#CM_SRC_GND      = 0
#CM_SRC_OSC      = 1
#CM_SRC_PLLD_PER = 6

; CM_*DIV, Table 100: PASSWD 31:24, DIVI 23:12, and the remaining 11:0
; are the fractional part - twelve bits, which is what the kernel calls
; CM_DIV_FRAC_BITS (clk-bcm2835.c:45).
#CM_DIV_INT_SHIFT = 12
#CM_DIV_INT_MAX   = 4095      ; twelve bits of DIVI

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PARENT CLOCK IS THE OSCILLATOR, AND ON THIS PART IT IS 54 MHz.
;
;  Not 19.2 MHz. bcm283x.dtsi:486-491 declares clk_osc at 19200000 for
;  the family and bcm2711.dtsi:618-620 OVERRIDES it:
;      &clk_osc { clock-frequency = <54000000>; };
;  The same 54 MHz turns up independently at the architectural counter,
;  which this tree has measured on the board (Lib/timer.pi4, CNTFRQ_EL0
;  read 54000000 on 2026-08-24). Two unrelated places agreeing is why
;  this constant is trusted rather than guessed.
;
;  WHY NOT PLLD, WHICH IS FASTER AND WHICH SRC=6 SELECTS. Because its
;  rate is not a number anywhere on this disk. PLLD's per output is
;  itself a programmable divider - A2W_PLLD_PER at 0x1540
;  (clk-bcm2835.c:274) - so using it means reading and modelling the PLL
;  to know what was asked for, and a parent rate that is wrong by a
;  factor produces a fan frequency that is wrong by the same factor with
;  no symptom this side of an oscilloscope. The oscillator's rate is
;  stated, cross-checked, and enough: 54 MHz reaches 25 kHz EXACTLY at
;  a divisor of 1 and a range of 2160, and covers 1 Hz upwards. PLLD
;  stays named in #CM_SRC_PLLD_PER and unused, so the day somebody needs
;  megahertz the constant is already correct.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM_OSC_HZ = 54000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FREQUENCY ENVELOPE, and every bound has a reason rather than a
;  round number.
;
;  #PWM_RANGE_TARGET is what the divisor search aims RNG at. It is the
;  duty resolution: a range of 2000 counts resolves a permille to two
;  counts, so every step the caller can ask for is a step the hardware
;  can make. Aiming higher costs nothing at low frequencies and is not
;  reachable at high ones, which is what the clamp below is for.
;
;  #PWM_RANGE_MIN is 2, and it is the kernel driver's PERIOD_MIN
;  (pwm-bcm2835.c:24) rather than a guess - a range of 1 leaves no room
;  for a duty at all and a range of 0 is a divide by zero in the part.
;
;  #PWM_RANGE_MAX is a twenty-bit range. RNG is a full 32-bit register
;  (Table 156) and nothing in the part objects to a larger one, but a
;  range beyond this means a period beyond twenty seconds at the slowest
;  divisor, which is not a PWM signal any more, and refusing it by name
;  is more useful than producing it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM_RANGE_TARGET = 2000
#PWM_RANGE_MIN    = 2
#PWM_RANGE_MAX    = 1048575

; The duty scale. Permille rather than percent because 25 kHz at a range
; of 2160 resolves far finer than one percent, and a fan curve that can
; only step in percent hunts at the bottom of its range.
#PWM_DUTY_MAX = 1000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RESULT CODES. Zero is success and every failure is negative and named;
;  no procedure here returns a bare 1 for "worked", because the caller of
;  PwmBegin needs to know WHICH refusal it hit to say a useful sentence.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM_OK        = 0
#PWM_ERR_PIN   = -1   ; not a pin this part can drive from a PWM channel
#PWM_ERR_HZ    = -2   ; a frequency clk_pwm cannot be divided down to
#PWM_ERR_DUTY  = -3   ; a duty outside 0..1000 permille
#PWM_ERR_BUSY  = -4   ; the clock generator would not stop
#PWM_ERR_STATE = -5   ; the channel was never begun
#PWM_ERR_SOFT  = -6   ; the software channel is already on another pin
#PWM_ERR_CLOCK = -7   ; the clock generator would not START - ENAB was
                      ; asserted and BUSY never rose. MEASURED ON THE
                      ; BOARD 2026-09-06: this is the failure that made
                      ; the whole driver report success and drive
                      ; nothing, and it had no code before that date.
#PWM_ERR_START = -8   ; the channel would not begin transmitting - the
                      ; control byte reads back correct and STAi stays
                      ; clear. See THE SILICON FACT below.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  KINDS. A caller that has been handed a pin needs to know which of the
;  two mechanisms it got, because their guarantees are not the same.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM_KIND_NONE = 0
#PWM_KIND_HARD = 1    ; a real PWM channel, clocked by the part
#PWM_KIND_SOFT = 2    ; a level toggled by software, coarse

; How long the clock generator is given to drop BUSY. The kernel gives
; every clock LOCK_TIMEOUT_NS (clk-bcm2835.c) and gives up with a
; message; this gives it a millisecond, which is four orders of
; magnitude longer than one cycle of the slowest clock this file will
; program, and then says so rather than writing into a running divider -
; Table 99: "To avoid lock-ups and glitches ... do not change this
; control while BUSY=1".
#PWM_BUSY_TIMEOUT_US = 1000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SILICON FACT, MEASURED ON THE BOARD 2026-09-06 (pi4-fan-silicon)
;
;  A CHANNEL MUST BE ENABLED WHILE ITS CLOCK GENERATOR IS STOPPED. It
;  starts when the generator starts. Enable it more than about a
;  hundred microseconds AFTER the generator is running and it never
;  starts at all, and NOTHING IN ANY REGISTER SAYS SO.
;
;  How that was found. `fan pin 18` + `fan 100` reported GPIO 18, ALT5,
;  25000 Hz, 54 MHz divided by 1, a range of 2160 and a duty of 100
;  percent. CM_PWMCTL read back $91 - BUSY, ENAB, source 1 - PWM0 CTL
;  read back $81 (MSEN1|PWEN1), RNG1 and DAT1 both read back $870. And
;  `gpio status 18` read ALT5 LEVEL 0, with PWM0 STA reading $102: BERR
;  set and STA1, "channel 1 is transmitting", CLEAR. Re-writing the
;  IDENTICAL control byte $81 brought the pad HIGH and STA1 with it
;  ($302). Re-writing RNG1 and DAT1 changed nothing.
;
;  Then a payload swept the gap between starting the generator and
;  writing the control byte, on the part, and STA1 came up at gaps of
;  0, 5, 20 and 100 microseconds and did NOT come up at 1000 - while
;  CM_PWMCTL read $91 the whole time, and while re-asserting the same
;  ENAB value changed nothing. Programming the channel FIRST and
;  starting the generator afterwards works with no gap and with a
;  millisecond of gap, which is the sequence this file now uses.
;
;  That is the behaviour of a block whose clock is gated away again once
;  no channel is asking for it. The datasheet does not say so; the part
;  does, five times out of five.
;
;  SO THE ORDER IS: stop the generator, set the divisor, set the source,
;  PROGRAM AND ENABLE THE CHANNEL, start the generator, wait for BUSY to
;  rise, then ask STAi whether it worked. The last step is what makes
;  this file unable to lie again: a channel that did not start is now an
;  error code and not a cheerful report.
;
;  A SECOND, SEPARATE WAY TO LOSE THE FAN, also measured: a generator
;  parked on SRC=GND never drops BUSY. CM_PWMCTL written $5A000000 read
;  back $00000080 and stayed; $5A000001 read back $00000001 at once. So
;  PwmClockStop names a real source rather than waiting forever.
;
;  #PWM_START_TIMEOUT_US is two milliseconds, and it is a floor rather
;  than a guess: two milliseconds is fifty periods at 25 kHz and it is
;  the window the bench payload used when it watched STA1 come up. The
;  programming burst itself needs no delays at all - the version with a
;  settle between every store is one of the versions that failed on the
;  bench - and the only thing this constant paces is the wait before the
;  recovery write, which must not arrive before the part has had its
;  chance to answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PWM_CLOCK_SETTLE_US   = 10
#PWM_START_TIMEOUT_US  = 2000
#PWM_START_TRIES       = 4

; ======================================================================
;  STATE
; ======================================================================
; The hardware side. One block/channel pair at a time is all a fan needs
; and all this file tracks; a second caller starting a second channel is
; supported by the register code below, but the reporting accessors
; describe the LAST channel begun. That is stated rather than hidden
; because a two-fan board would want an array here and would notice.
Global pwm_pin.i          = -1        ; the pin currently driven, or -1
Global pwm_base.i         = 0         ; #PWM0_BASE or #PWM1_BASE
Global pwm_chan.i         = -1        ; 0 for channel 1, 1 for channel 2
Global pwm_range.i        = 0         ; RNG as programmed
Global pwm_divi.i         = 0         ; DIVI as programmed
Global pwm_wantHz.i       = 0         ; what was asked for
Global pwm_actualHz.i     = 0         ; what the divisors actually make
Global pwm_duty.i         = 0         ; permille as last set
Global pwm_kind.i         = #PWM_KIND_NONE
Global pwm_startWrites.i  = 0         ; control-byte writes the LAST
                                      ; PwmBegin needed before STAi came
                                      ; up. One means the part behaved.

; The software side. ONE channel, deliberately: there is one fan, a
; second software channel doubles the work done inside somebody else's
; tick, and two of them sharing a service call would beat against each
; other. A second request is refused by name (#PWM_ERR_SOFT) rather than
; silently moving the first one.
Global pwmsoft_pin.i      = -1
Global pwmsoft_periodUs.i = 0
Global pwmsoft_highUs.i   = 0
Global pwmsoft_markUs.i   = 0         ; when the current period started
Global pwmsoft_level.i    = 0         ; what the pad was last driven to
Global pwmsoft_idle.i     = 1         ; where a stall parks the pin
Global pwmsoft_stalls.i   = 0         ; how many times it has parked
Global pwmsoft_edges.i    = 0         ; edges driven, for the bench

; The software channel's frequency ceiling. Its service call runs from
; somebody else's periodic tick, so the shortest period it can honour is
; a few times that tick's own interval; a millisecond period asks the
; caller for a service call every few hundred microseconds. Above this
; the answer would be a waveform whose duty is decided by scheduling
; jitter, so it is refused instead.
#PWM_SOFT_HZ_MIN = 1
#PWM_SOFT_HZ_MAX = 1000

; A service call that arrives this many periods late means the caller
; stopped calling - a long command at the prompt, a transfer, a hang.
; The pin is parked at its idle level and the event is counted. Two is
; the smallest number that cannot be reached by ordinary jitter and is
; still noticed within a few milliseconds at the default frequency.
#PWM_SOFT_STALL_PERIODS = 2

; ======================================================================
;  THE PIN TABLE
; ======================================================================
;  Section 8.5's Quick Reference lists the GPIOs assigned to PWM
;  channels, but its table in the extracted text is a column layout that
;  can be read two ways. The device tree states the same pairing one pin
;  at a time and cannot be misread, so it is the source here and the
;  section is the corroboration:
;
;    Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi
;      842  pwm0_0_gpio12  gpio12  alt0        PWM0 channel 1
;      849  pwm0_0_gpio18  gpio18  alt5        PWM0 channel 1
;      856  pwm1_0_gpio40  gpio40  alt0        PWM1 channel 1
;      863  pwm0_1_gpio13  gpio13  alt0        PWM0 channel 2
;      870  pwm0_1_gpio19  gpio19  alt5        PWM0 channel 2
;      877  pwm1_1_gpio41  gpio41  alt0        PWM1 channel 2
;      884  pwm0_1_gpio45  gpio45  alt0        PWM0 channel 2
;      891  pwm0_0_gpio52  gpio52  alt1        PWM0 channel 1
;      898  pwm0_1_gpio53  gpio53  alt1        PWM0 channel 2
;
;  The node names carry the mapping in themselves: pwm<block>_<channel>
;  _gpio<pin>. So gpio18 is block 0 channel 0 - which Table 152 calls
;  channel 1 - on alternate function 5, and that is the pin most Pi
;  fan wiring uses.
;
;  ONLY FOUR OF THE NINE REACH THE 40-PIN HEADER: 12, 13, 18 and 19.
;  40, 41, 45, 52 and 53 are real die lines with on-board uses and are
;  above the header's range; the board backend is what decides which to
;  offer an operator, not this file, which answers for the silicon.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; PwmPinBlock(pin) - #PWM0_BASE, #PWM1_BASE, or 0 for a pin with no
; PWM function at all.
Procedure.i PwmPinBlock(pin.i)
  Select pin
    Case 12
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
    Case 13
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
    Case 18
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
    Case 19
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
    Case 40
      ProcedureReturn #PWM1_BASE
    Case 41
      ProcedureReturn #PWM1_BASE
    Case 45
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
    Case 52
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
    Case 53
      ProcedureReturn #PWM0_BASE
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; PwmPinChannel(pin) - 0 for the block's channel 1, 1 for its channel 2,
; -1 for a pin with no PWM function. The names in the device tree are
; zero-based (pwm0_0, pwm0_1) and Table 152's registers are one-based
; (RNG1, RNG2); this returns the zero-based index and every register
; address below is computed from it, so the two numberings meet in one
; place instead of at every call site.
Procedure.i PwmPinChannel(pin.i)
  Select pin
    Case 12
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 13
      ProcedureReturn 1
    Case 18
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 19
      ProcedureReturn 1
    Case 40
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 41
      ProcedureReturn 1
    Case 45
      ProcedureReturn 1
    Case 52
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 53
      ProcedureReturn 1
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; PwmPinAlt(pin) - the alternate function number that puts the PWM
; channel on the pad, or -1. Three different answers across nine pins,
; which is exactly why this is a table and not a constant.
Procedure.i PwmPinAlt(pin.i)
  Select pin
    Case 12
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 13
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 18
      ProcedureReturn 5
    Case 19
      ProcedureReturn 5
    Case 40
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 41
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 45
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case 52
      ProcedureReturn 1
    Case 53
      ProcedureReturn 1
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; PwmPinIsCapable(pin) - 1 if the part can drive a hardware PWM channel
; out of this pin.
Procedure.i PwmPinIsCapable(pin.i)
  If PwmPinBlock(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  REGISTER ADDRESSES
; ======================================================================

; The channel's range register: RNG1 at 0x10 and RNG2 at 0x20, so the
; stride between the two channels' register pairs is 0x10 - which is
; also how the kernel driver writes it, `PERIOD(x) (((x) * 0x10) + 0x10)`
; (pwm-bcm2835.c:21). Both derivations are written here because the two
; agreeing is the check.
Procedure.i PwmRngAddr(base.i, chan.i)
  ProcedureReturn base + #PWM_RNG1_OFF + chan * $10
EndProcedure

; DAT1 at 0x14, DAT2 at 0x24; the same stride.
; `DUTY(x) (((x) * 0x10) + 0x14)` - pwm-bcm2835.c:22.
Procedure.i PwmDatAddr(base.i, chan.i)
  ProcedureReturn base + #PWM_DAT1_OFF + chan * $10
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE DIVISOR SEARCH
; ======================================================================
;  Given a wanted frequency, choose DIVI and RNG so that
;
;      f = #PWM_OSC_HZ / (DIVI * RNG)
;
;  with RNG as close to #PWM_RANGE_TARGET as the bounds allow, because
;  RNG is the duty resolution and DIVI is not.
;
;  It is a division, not a search: the DIVI that puts RNG at the target
;  is osc/(f * target), and clamping it to 1..4095 and recomputing RNG
;  handles both ends. Then RNG is checked against its own bounds, which
;  is where a frequency that cannot be made gets refused.
;
;  WORKED, because these are the numbers the gate checks:
;    25000 Hz  DIVI = 54e6/(25000*2000) = 1 (truncated from 1.08)
;              RNG  = 54e6/(1*25000)    = 2160
;              f    = 54e6/(1*2160)     = 25000   exactly
;     1000 Hz  DIVI = 27, RNG = 2000, f = 1000    exactly
;      100 Hz  DIVI = 270, RNG = 2000, f = 100    exactly
;  and at the top:
;    27000000  DIVI = 1, RNG = 2, f = 27000000    exactly, and one more
;              hertz refuses with #PWM_ERR_HZ because RNG would be 1.
;
;  THE TRUNCATION IS DELIBERATE AND IT IS WHY 25 kHz IS EXACT. Rounding
;  DIVI up to 2 would give RNG = 1080 and the same exact frequency with
;  half the duty resolution. Truncating biases DIVI down, which biases
;  RNG up, which is the direction that costs nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; PwmDivisorFor(hz) - the DIVI this file would use. Also the first half
; of the refusal test, so it is a procedure rather than three lines
; inside PwmBegin: the gate calls it directly.
Procedure.i PwmDivisorFor(hz.i)
  Define d.i
  If hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The product cannot overflow: hz is bounded below by the caller's own
  ; check and the widest sensible operand here is 27 million times two
  ; thousand, which is 5.4e10 - inside a 64-bit .i by nine orders of
  ; magnitude. On a 32-bit target this line would need splitting, which
  ; is one more reason this file is a Pi 4 file.
  d = #PWM_OSC_HZ / (hz * #PWM_RANGE_TARGET)
  If d < 1
    d = 1
  EndIf
  If d > #CM_DIV_INT_MAX
    d = #CM_DIV_INT_MAX
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn d
EndProcedure

; PwmRangeFor(hz) - the RNG that goes with PwmDivisorFor(hz), or 0 if
; the frequency is out of the envelope. 0 is not a range the part can
; use, so it doubles as the refusal without a second out-parameter.
Procedure.i PwmRangeFor(hz.i)
  Define d.i
  Define r.i
  If hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  d = PwmDivisorFor(hz)
  If d < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  r = #PWM_OSC_HZ / (d * hz)
  If r < #PWM_RANGE_MIN
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If r > #PWM_RANGE_MAX
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; PwmActualHzFor(hz) - the frequency the part will really produce for a
; request, or 0 if the request is out of the envelope. The caller prints
; THIS, never the number it asked for: at 25 kHz they are the same and
; at 7 Hz they are not, and a report that says 7 when the fan sees 7.0007
; is the kind of confident wrong answer this project keeps finding.
Procedure.i PwmActualHzFor(hz.i)
  Define d.i
  Define r.i
  r = PwmRangeFor(hz)
  If r = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  d = PwmDivisorFor(hz)
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OSC_HZ / (d * r)
EndProcedure

; PwmDutyCounts(range, permille) - the DAT value for a duty, rounded to
; nearest rather than truncated. Half a count of bias at a range of 2160
; is 0.023 percent and would not matter; it matters at a range of 4,
; where truncation turns a 749-permille request into one count of eight
; and rounding turns it into three.
;
; The two rails are exact by construction: 0 permille gives 0 counts and
; 1000 permille gives exactly `range` counts, which with MSEN=1 is a
; solid level rather than a period with one missing edge.
Procedure.i PwmDutyCounts(range.i, permille.i)
  If permille <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If permille >= #PWM_DUTY_MAX
    ProcedureReturn range
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (range * permille + #PWM_DUTY_MAX / 2) / #PWM_DUTY_MAX
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CLOCK GENERATOR
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i PwmCmRead(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#CM_BASE + off) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; Every write to either register carries the password in 31:24. There is
; one procedure so that there is one place the password can be missing
; from, and it cannot be missing from that one - the argument is the
; VALUE, and the password is added here.
Procedure PwmCmWrite(off.i, value.i)
  PokeN(#CM_BASE + off, #CM_PASSWD | (value & $00FFFFFF))
EndProcedure

; PwmClockStop() - drop ENAB and wait for BUSY to fall.
;
; Table 99 on ENAB: "This requests the clock to start or stop without
; glitches. The output clock will not stop immediately because the cycle
; must be allowed to complete ... The BUSY flag will go low when the
; final cycle is completed." So the wait is not optional politeness; the
; divider must not be written while BUSY is set, and the same table says
; that in as many words for MASH, SRC and DIVI.
;
; The kernel does exactly this pair - clear CM_ENABLE, then
; bcm2835_clock_wait_busy (clk-bcm2835.c:1088-1101) - and gives up with
; a message on timeout. This returns #PWM_ERR_BUSY instead of writing
; into a running generator.
;
; ONE RECOVERY, AND IT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED. A generator
; whose SRC field is GND does not stop: on the board on 2026-09-06,
; CM_PWMCTL written to $5A000000 (password, SRC=GND, ENAB clear) read
; back $00000080 - BUSY set - and STAYED there. Writing $5A000001, the
; same register with the oscillator selected and ENAB still clear, read
; back $00000001 immediately. So a stuck BUSY whose source is GND is
; cured by naming a real source, and this is the one place that knows
; it. Without this the whole command is a permanent refusal - "that pin
; could not be set up as a fan output" - after anything at all leaves
; the generator parked on GND, which is how this failure was first
; reproduced from the prompt.
Procedure.i PwmClockStop()
  Define v.i
  Define t0.i
  Define tries.i
  v = PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF)
  PwmCmWrite(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF, v & ~#CM_CTL_ENAB)
  tries = 0
  While tries < 2
    t0 = Micros()
    While (PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF) & #CM_CTL_BUSY) <> 0
      If Micros() - t0 > #PWM_BUSY_TIMEOUT_US
        Break
      EndIf
    Wend
    If (PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF) & #CM_CTL_BUSY) = 0
      ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
    EndIf
    If (PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF) & #CM_CTL_SRC_MASK) <> #CM_SRC_GND
      ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_BUSY
    EndIf
    PwmCmWrite(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF, #CM_SRC_OSC)
    tries = tries + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_BUSY
EndProcedure

; PwmClockPrepare(divi) - steps 1 to 3 of the clock start: stop, program
; the divisor, select the source. IT DOES NOT ENABLE. The ORDER IS THE
; WHOLE PROCEDURE and it comes straight out of Table 99's two warnings:
; do not change MASH or SRC while BUSY is set, and do not change them
; "at the same time as asserting ENAB". So source and enable are two
; separate writes, not one.
;
;   1  stop and wait                          (PwmClockStop)
;   2  DIV  = PASSWD | DIVI<<12 | 0           integer division, no frac
;   3  CTL  = PASSWD | MASH 0 | SRC           source chosen, still off
;   4  CTL  = PASSWD | MASH 0 | SRC | ENAB | GATE   -- PwmClockRun()
;
; STEP 4 IS A SEPARATE PROCEDURE BECAUSE THE CHANNEL GOES BETWEEN THEM.
; See THE SILICON FACT at the top of this file: on this part the channel
; has to be programmed and enabled while the generator is still stopped,
; and it starts when the generator does.
;
; MASH IS ZERO, WHICH ALSO SETTLES A DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE SOURCES
; RATHER THAN CHOOSING BETWEEN THEM. Table 99 calls bits 10:9 MASH, with
; 0 meaning integer division; the kernel calls bit 9 CM_FRAC and sets it
; only when the fractional part of the divisor is non-zero
; (clk-bcm2835.c:1161-1162). This file divides by an integer, so DIVF is
; zero, so bit 9 is zero under both readings and nothing here has to
; decide which is right.
;
; GATE, bit 6, is Reserved in Table 99 and BIT(6) in the kernel, which
; sets it together with ENABLE for every per-peripheral clock
; (clk-bcm2835.c:1111-1114). The kernel is the code that runs this block
; on this part every day, so it is followed, and the discrepancy is
; recorded here rather than quietly resolved.
Procedure.i PwmClockPrepare(divi.i)
  Define rc.i
  If divi < 1
    divi = 1
  EndIf
  If divi > #CM_DIV_INT_MAX
    divi = #CM_DIV_INT_MAX
  EndIf
  rc = PwmClockStop()
  If rc <> #PWM_OK
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf
  PwmCmWrite(#CM_PWMDIV_OFF, divi << #CM_DIV_INT_SHIFT)
  PwmCmWrite(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF, #CM_SRC_OSC)
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; PwmClockRun() - step 4, on its own, because a whole channel has to be
; programmed between it and step 3. Assert ENAB and then WAIT FOR BUSY
; TO RISE.
;
; Table 99 calls ENAB a request - "This requests the clock to start or
; stop without glitches" - and BUSY the answer. The wait is not
; politeness: it is what makes the STA check that follows it mean
; anything, because a channel read for its state before its clock is up
; answers "not transmitting" whether or not it was going to run.
Procedure.i PwmClockRun()
  Define t0.i
  PwmCmWrite(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF, #CM_SRC_OSC | #CM_CTL_ENAB | #CM_CTL_GATE)
  t0 = Micros()
  While (PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF) & #CM_CTL_BUSY) = 0
    If Micros() - t0 > #PWM_BUSY_TIMEOUT_US
      ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_CLOCK
    EndIf
  Wend
  delayMicroseconds(#PWM_CLOCK_SETTLE_US)
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; PwmClockHz() - what clk_pwm is running at, read back from the
; registers rather than remembered. Zero when the generator is not
; enabled, because a rate for a stopped clock is a fiction.
Procedure.i PwmClockHz()
  Define ctl.i
  Define divi.i
  ctl = PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMCTL_OFF)
  If (ctl & #CM_CTL_ENAB) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (ctl & #CM_CTL_SRC_MASK) <> #CM_SRC_OSC
    ; Somebody else's source. The rate is not knowable from here without
    ; a PLL model, so say nothing rather than report the oscillator's.
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  divi = (PwmCmRead(#CM_PWMDIV_OFF) >> #CM_DIV_INT_SHIFT) & #CM_DIV_INT_MAX
  If divi < 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OSC_HZ / divi
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE HARDWARE CHANNEL
; ======================================================================

; PwmCtlWrite(base, chan, byte) - replace one channel's control byte and
; leave the other channel's alone. Read-modify-write, which is safe here
; only because a single caller owns this block; two cores starting two
; channels at once would need the same treatment GPSET/GPCLR gets, and
; the part offers no set/clear pair for CTL.
;
; CLRF (bit 6) is masked OUT of whatever is written, always. It is the
; shared FIFO's one-shot and this file uses no FIFO; letting it through
; a per-channel byte would make channel 2's SBIT position clear channel
; 1's FIFO, which is the exact confusion the CTL note at the top warns
; about.
Procedure PwmCtlWrite(base.i, chan.i, byte.i)
  Define v.i
  Define sh.i
  sh = chan * #PWM_CTL_CHAN_SHIFT
  v = PeekN(base + #PWM_CTL_OFF) & $FFFFFFFF
  v = v & ~(#PWM_CTL_CHAN_MASK << sh)
  v = v & ~#PWM_CTL_CLRF
  v = v | ((byte & #PWM_CTL_CHAN_MASK) << sh)
  v = v & ~#PWM_CTL_CLRF
  PokeN(base + #PWM_CTL_OFF, v)
EndProcedure

; PwmStaRead(base) - the block's status word, as a positive number.
;
; THE ONLY REGISTER IN THIS FILE THAT REPORTS ON THE PART RATHER THAN ON
; THIS FILE'S OWN WRITES. CTL, RNG and DAT all read back what was
; written to them whether or not the channel ever started - measured, on
; the board, with the pad low (THE SILICON FACT above). STAi is the one
; bit that comes from the channel itself.
Procedure.i PwmStaRead(base.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(base + #PWM_STA_OFF) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; PwmChanStaMask(chan) - STA1 for channel index 0, STA2 for index 1.
; Table 154 puts them at bits 9 and 10, one apart, so the shift is the
; channel index and not the index times anything.
Procedure.i PwmChanStaMask(chan.i)
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_STA_STA1 << chan
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PwmProgramChannel(base, chan, rng) - the four channel stores, made
;  WHILE THE CLOCK GENERATOR IS STILL STOPPED.
;
;  That is the whole of the fix of 2026-09-06 and it is measured, not
;  reasoned: with the generator running, this same burst leaves STA1
;  clear and the pad low while every register reads back perfect, and
;  with the generator stopped the channel is transmitting the moment the
;  generator starts. A gap of a millisecond between this and the start
;  does not hurt; a gap of a millisecond between a start and this one
;  does. See THE SILICON FACT.
;
;  BERR (Table 154) is write-one-to-clear and is cleared first, so a
;  BERR read after this burst belongs to this burst.
;
;  THE ORDER INSIDE THE BURST STILL MATTERS for the reason it always
;  did: the channel is disabled, then RNG and DAT are written, then it
;  is enabled. Table 153 says "All registers and FIFOs are writeable
;  without setting this bit", so writing them while disabled is allowed;
;  the other order would run at the reset range of 32 (Table 156).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PwmProgramChannel(base.i, chan.i, rng.i)
  PokeN(base + #PWM_STA_OFF, #PWM_STA_BERR)
  PwmCtlWrite(base, chan, 0)                        ; channel off
  PokeN(PwmRngAddr(base, chan), rng)
  PokeN(PwmDatAddr(base, chan), 0)
  PwmCtlWrite(base, chan, #PWM_CTL_MSEN | #PWM_CTL_PWEN)
  pwm_startWrites = 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PwmConfirmChannel(base, chan) - ask the CHANNEL whether it is running,
;  and refuse rather than report if it is not.
;
;  STAi is the only bit in this block that a driver cannot make up by
;  writing to it, which is why the check is here and not on CTL. The
;  retry is the recovery that was measured to work on a channel that had
;  missed its start: a second write of the IDENTICAL control byte. It is
;  counted, because a board that needs it every time is telling you
;  something about its clock and should not be able to say it quietly.
;
;  A channel that will not start leaves the pad UNMUXED and the channel
;  disabled - the caller gets a named refusal and a pin that was never
;  touched, which is the outcome the first version of this file could
;  not produce.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PwmConfirmChannel(base.i, chan.i)
  Define sta.i
  Define tries.i
  Define t0.i
  sta = PwmChanStaMask(chan)
  tries = 0
  While tries < #PWM_START_TRIES
    ; WAIT FOR IT BEFORE TOUCHING IT AGAIN. A version of this procedure
    ; that read STA once and then re-wrote the control byte KILLED the
    ; channel it was checking on: the first read is a few microseconds
    ; after the generator came up, STA1 is not up yet, and the re-write
    ; lands on a running block, which is the very thing that does not
    ; start a channel. Four of those in a row leave STA $102 - BERR set,
    ; STA1 clear - which is exactly what the bench measured before this
    ; loop learned to be patient.
    t0 = Micros()
    While Micros() - t0 <= #PWM_START_TIMEOUT_US
      If (PwmStaRead(base) & sta) <> 0
        ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
      EndIf
    Wend
    PwmCtlWrite(base, chan, #PWM_CTL_MSEN | #PWM_CTL_PWEN)
    pwm_startWrites = pwm_startWrites + 1
    tries = tries + 1
  Wend
  If (PwmStaRead(base) & sta) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
  EndIf
  PwmCtlWrite(base, chan, 0)
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_START
EndProcedure

; PwmBegin(pin, hz) - put a real PWM channel on a pin at a frequency.
;
; ORDER MATTERS TWICE HERE.
;
;   The channel is DISABLED, then RNG and DAT are written, then it is
;   enabled. Table 153 says "All registers and FIFOs are writeable
;   without setting this bit", so writing them while disabled is allowed;
;   doing it in the other order would run the channel at the reset range
;   of 32 (Table 156) for however long the two stores take, which at the
;   oscillator is a 1.7 MHz burst on a pin somebody is about to connect
;   a fan to.
;
;   The PIN IS MUXED LAST. Until PinAlt runs the pad is whatever it was;
;   after it, the pad follows the channel. Muxing first would put the
;   reset-range burst on the wire.
;
; The duty starts at 0. A caller that wanted a fan spinning asks for it
; on the next line, and a library that guessed would be the reason a
; board came up with a fan at full speed.
Procedure.i PwmBegin(pin.i, hz.i)
  Define base.i
  Define chan.i
  Define alt.i
  Define rng.i
  Define divi.i
  Define rc.i

  base = PwmPinBlock(pin)
  If base = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_PIN
  EndIf
  chan = PwmPinChannel(pin)
  alt  = PwmPinAlt(pin)
  If chan < 0 Or alt < 0
    ; Unreachable while the three tables agree, and checked anyway: a
    ; pin present in one table and missing from another is a defect in
    ; this file, and it should fail here rather than compute an address
    ; from a negative channel index.
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_PIN
  EndIf

  rng = PwmRangeFor(hz)
  If rng = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_HZ
  EndIf
  divi = PwmDivisorFor(hz)

  ; THE THREE STEPS, IN THE ORDER THE PART DEMANDS. Swapping the middle
  ; two is the defect this file was born with: see THE SILICON FACT.
  rc = PwmClockPrepare(divi)
  If rc <> #PWM_OK
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf

  GpioBarrier()
  PwmProgramChannel(base, chan, rng)
  rc = PwmClockRun()
  GpioBarrier()
  If rc <> #PWM_OK
    PwmCtlWrite(base, chan, 0)
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf

  ; AND THEN THE PART'S OWN ANSWER. Everything written above reads back
  ; correct on a channel that never started; STAi does not.
  rc = PwmConfirmChannel(base, chan)
  If rc <> #PWM_OK
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf

  PinAlt(pin, alt)

  pwm_pin      = pin
  pwm_base     = base
  pwm_chan     = chan
  pwm_range    = rng
  pwm_divi     = divi
  pwm_wantHz   = hz
  pwm_actualHz = #PWM_OSC_HZ / (divi * rng)
  pwm_duty     = 0
  pwm_kind     = #PWM_KIND_HARD
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; PwmDuty(permille) - the duty of the channel PwmBegin started.
;
; A duty outside 0..1000 is REFUSED rather than clamped. Clamping is the
; friendlier behaviour right up to the moment a caller's arithmetic goes
; wrong by a factor of ten and the fan runs flat out with nothing in any
; log saying anything happened.
Procedure.i PwmDuty(permille.i)
  If pwm_kind <> #PWM_KIND_HARD
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_STATE
  EndIf
  If permille < 0 Or permille > #PWM_DUTY_MAX
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_DUTY
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  PokeN(PwmDatAddr(pwm_base, pwm_chan), PwmDutyCounts(pwm_range, permille))
  GpioBarrier()
  pwm_duty = permille
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; PwmEnd() - stop the channel and hand the pin back as an INPUT.
;
; An input, not an output driven low, and that is a safety decision
; rather than tidiness: a 2-wire fan on a transistor is off when the
; gate is not driven, and a 4-pin fan reads an undriven PWM input as
; "no control signal", which every specification of that connector
; answers with FULL SPEED. Either way the fan ends up in its safe state
; and not in whichever one the last DAT write happened to leave.
;
; The clock generator is left RUNNING. It is shared by both blocks and
; all four channels; stopping it here would stop a second channel this
; file does not know about. Stopping the channel stops the output.
Procedure.i PwmEnd()
  If pwm_kind <> #PWM_KIND_HARD
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_STATE
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  PwmCtlWrite(pwm_base, pwm_chan, 0)
  PokeN(PwmDatAddr(pwm_base, pwm_chan), 0)
  GpioBarrier()
  PinInput(pwm_pin)
  pwm_pin      = -1
  pwm_base     = 0
  pwm_chan     = -1
  pwm_range    = 0
  pwm_divi     = 0
  pwm_wantHz   = 0
  pwm_actualHz = 0
  pwm_duty     = 0
  pwm_kind     = #PWM_KIND_NONE
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE SOFTWARE CHANNEL
; ======================================================================
;  For a 2-wire fan on a transistor on a pin with no PWM function. It
;  toggles a plain output from a service call the caller makes as often
;  as it can, and it is COARSE - not "slightly less accurate", coarse:
;
;    * its edges land wherever the next service call lands, so the duty
;      is right on average over many periods and wrong on any one;
;    * a command that runs for a second without servicing it leaves the
;      pin wherever the last edge put it;
;    * it cannot go above #PWM_SOFT_HZ_MAX and refuses to try.
;
;  THE STALL RULE IS WHY IT IS SAFE ANYWAY. Every service call measures
;  how long since the last one. Past #PWM_SOFT_STALL_PERIODS periods it
;  stops modulating, drives the pin to its IDLE level and counts the
;  event, so a long-running command parks the fan at the level the
;  caller nominated as safe - full on, for a fan - rather than at a
;  coin toss. The count is reported, because a fan that spends its life
;  parked is a fan that should be on a PWM pin instead.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; PwmSoftPeriodUs(hz) - the period in microseconds, or 0 for a frequency
; outside the software channel's envelope.
Procedure.i PwmSoftPeriodUs(hz.i)
  If hz < #PWM_SOFT_HZ_MIN Or hz > #PWM_SOFT_HZ_MAX
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1000000 / hz
EndProcedure

; PwmSoftDrive(level) - the one place the pad is written, so the shadow
; and the pin cannot disagree and the edge count is honest.
Procedure PwmSoftDrive(level.i)
  If level <> pwmsoft_level
    pwmsoft_edges = pwmsoft_edges + 1
  EndIf
  pwmsoft_level = level
  If level <> 0
    PinHigh(pwmsoft_pin)
  Else
    PinLow(pwmsoft_pin)
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; PwmSoftBegin(pin, hz, idleLevel) - claim a pin for software PWM.
;
; The pin is staged LOW and then made an output, the same order
; Lib/gpio.pi4's PinHigh note describes and the same order the `gpio`
; command uses, so the pad does not glitch through whatever the last
; owner left in GPSET/GPCLR.
Procedure.i PwmSoftBegin(pin.i, hz.i, idleLevel.i)
  Define per.i
  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_PIN
  EndIf
  If pwmsoft_pin >= 0 And pwmsoft_pin <> pin
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_SOFT
  EndIf
  per = PwmSoftPeriodUs(hz)
  If per = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_HZ
  EndIf
  pwmsoft_pin      = pin
  pwmsoft_periodUs = per
  pwmsoft_highUs   = 0
  pwmsoft_markUs   = Micros()
  pwmsoft_idle     = idleLevel
  pwmsoft_stalls   = 0
  pwmsoft_edges    = 0
  ; THE PIN IS STAGED LOW AND THEN MADE AN OUTPUT, the same order
  ; Lib/gpio.pi4's PinHigh note describes and the same order the `gpio`
  ; command uses, so the pad does not glitch through whatever the last
  ; owner left in GPSET/GPCLR.
  PinLow(pin)
  PinOutput(pin)
  ; The shadow is set to the OPPOSITE of what is about to be driven so
  ; that the first PwmSoftDrive counts a real edge. Without it a channel
  ; that begins at zero and stays there would report no edges at all and
  ; look identical to one that was never serviced.
  pwmsoft_level    = 1
  PwmSoftDrive(0)
  pwm_pin      = pin
  pwm_wantHz   = hz
  pwm_actualHz = 1000000 / per
  pwm_duty     = 0
  pwm_kind     = #PWM_KIND_SOFT
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; PwmSoftDuty(permille) - the fraction of each period the pin is high.
Procedure.i PwmSoftDuty(permille.i)
  If pwm_kind <> #PWM_KIND_SOFT
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_STATE
  EndIf
  If permille < 0 Or permille > #PWM_DUTY_MAX
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_DUTY
  EndIf
  pwmsoft_highUs = PwmDutyCounts(pwmsoft_periodUs, permille)
  pwm_duty = permille
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; PwmSoftService() - drive the pin for right now. Call it as often as
; possible; it is a subtraction and a compare when there is nothing to
; do. Returns 1 if it drove an edge, 0 otherwise, so a caller that wants
; to know whether it is keeping up can find out.
;
; THE RAILS ARE HANDLED BEFORE THE PHASE ARITHMETIC, not by it. A duty
; of 0 with a period boundary in the past would otherwise drive high for
; the zero microseconds between the two compares - a runt pulse at every
; period, on a rail that is supposed to be a solid level.
Procedure.i PwmSoftService()
  Define now.i
  Define since.i
  If pwm_kind <> #PWM_KIND_SOFT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  now = Micros()
  since = now - pwmsoft_markUs

  ; THE STALL RULE. A gap this long is not jitter, it is a caller that
  ; stopped calling; park the pin and re-anchor so the first period
  ; after the stall is a whole one rather than a fragment.
  If since > pwmsoft_periodUs * #PWM_SOFT_STALL_PERIODS
    pwmsoft_stalls = pwmsoft_stalls + 1
    pwmsoft_markUs = now
    PwmSoftDrive(pwmsoft_idle)
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; Advance to the current period. A While rather than an If because a
  ; single late call can be more than one period late and still inside
  ; the stall bound, and stepping the mark by one period at a time keeps
  ; the phase rather than resetting it.
  While since >= pwmsoft_periodUs
    pwmsoft_markUs = pwmsoft_markUs + pwmsoft_periodUs
    since = since - pwmsoft_periodUs
  Wend

  If pwmsoft_highUs <= 0
    PwmSoftDrive(0)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If pwmsoft_highUs >= pwmsoft_periodUs
    PwmSoftDrive(1)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If since < pwmsoft_highUs
    PwmSoftDrive(1)
  Else
    PwmSoftDrive(0)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; PwmSoftEnd() - release the pin, as an input, for the reason PwmEnd
; gives.
Procedure.i PwmSoftEnd()
  If pwm_kind <> #PWM_KIND_SOFT
    ProcedureReturn #PWM_ERR_STATE
  EndIf
  PinInput(pwmsoft_pin)
  pwmsoft_pin      = -1
  pwmsoft_periodUs = 0
  pwmsoft_highUs   = 0
  pwmsoft_level    = 0
  pwm_pin      = -1
  pwm_wantHz   = 0
  pwm_actualHz = 0
  pwm_duty     = 0
  pwm_kind     = #PWM_KIND_NONE
  ProcedureReturn #PWM_OK
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  WHAT IS RUNNING - read by the command that has to report it
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i PwmKind()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_kind
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmPin()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_pin
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmHz()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_actualHz
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmRequestedHz()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_wantHz
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmDutyNow()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_duty
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmRange()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_range
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmDivisor()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_divi
EndProcedure

; PwmStartWrites() - how many control-byte writes the last PwmBegin
; needed before the channel answered. One is the part behaving; more is
; the clock-domain crossing that THE SILICON FACT describes, and it is
; reported rather than hidden.
Procedure.i PwmStartWrites()
  ProcedureReturn pwm_startWrites
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmSoftStalls()
  ProcedureReturn pwmsoft_stalls
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PwmSoftEdges()
  ProcedureReturn pwmsoft_edges
EndProcedure

; PwmSoftLevel() - the shadow, not the pad. The pad is GPLEV's to answer
; and DigitalRead is how a caller asks; this says what this file last
; drove, which is the useful half when the two disagree.
Procedure.i PwmSoftLevel()
  ProcedureReturn pwmsoft_level
EndProcedure
